From e5da26f75402e17a91bddaf22e9f736ada04a77e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fidecastro <18445184+fidecastro@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 16:17:52 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] fix(import): accept NV12/P010 DMA-BUFs whose chroma plane is
 off the row grid

import_prime() required the chroma offset to be a whole number of luma
rows after the luma offset, because Memory describes chroma as
data_offset + stride * storage_height. Mesa's freedreno places the
chroma plane of a linear NV12 buffer at the next page boundary after
luma (fdl6_layout_multiplanar_image: align64(y_layout->size, 4096)).
For 1920x1080 that is offset 2076672, 1081.6 rows, so vaCreateSurfaces
failed with "unsupported DMA-BUF plane layout" and Chromium's VA-API
encoder, which allocates its input through GBM, could not encode 1080p.
1280x720 and 640x480 only worked because their luma size is already a
multiple of the page size.

Give Memory a chroma_skew, the bytes between the last whole row slot
and the chroma plane, and a chroma() accessor, and use it wherever the
chroma position was computed. A skewed buffer never takes the direct
encoder path; it is staged like any other import whose layout differs
from the encoder's.

Tested on a Surface Laptop 7 15-inch (X1E80100, kernel 7.3) with a
program that imports a DMA-BUF with a chosen chroma offset, fills it
through vaDeriveImage and encodes one H.264 High IDR frame: the
1920x1080 page-aligned layout was refused before and now encodes to the
same bytes as the 1088-row and 1080-row layouts (49.3 dB luma PSNR
against the source); 1280x720 output is unchanged. tests/decoder/
prime-import gains a skewed case; it needs the DMA heap and was only
compiled here.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2
